Dasein wrote:
>
> OK I’m a bit of a Nethack noob, only recently started playing,

You're also a newbie to newsgroups.  There's a tradtion on UseNet to
spell out words.  Avoid the abbrevs as you type.  ;^)

> Very early on (D lvl 2 I think?) I found an armour shop, and my dog
> grabbed for me boots and gloves, which turned out to be +3 boots, and
> gauntlets of power. Rock on.

Keep BUC checking armor items with your pet or at an altar.  Try on any
item you prove isn't cursed.  Build up your AC and get as many armor
items as you can wear.  EArly on that includes a shield but any class
capable of dual wield eventually wants to drop the shield in favor of
the greater damage of dual wield.

> On D lvl 3 or so I found an aligned
> altar, though have had bad luck so far with corpses.

Bad luck converting or not getting gifts?  Every XP level makes
converting an altar more likely.  I start trying at XP=5.  For artifact
gifts count the sacrfices.  It basically takes a number with more digits
than you expect to get artifact gifts.  The number grows geometerically
with the number of existing artifacts after you get the first one.

> I’m currently on
> D lvl 5, I’m exp lvl 4. My dog got separated from me and killed by
> something  But I figure it’s not a huge deal, since with STR 25 and a
> battle-axe and AC 3 I’m killing things effortlessly, and I can use the
> altar to BUC test stuff.

BUC testing isn't the only advantage of pets but pet management skills
are something to learn later at this point.

> So what’s the best plan now? From what I gather the usual plan is
> Sokoban, then mines down to mine’s end, then back to main dungeon
> branch.

Various orders of mines and Sokoban all work.  I currently like to do
the mines to minetown because the crowds of monsters help me level up
quickly in the early one-digit numbers.  Then Sokoban then back into the
mines for mines end.  If I stay in the main branch it's a lot of work to
stay fed.  Doing the mines early has me enter Sokoban Satiated and that
lets me keep the food hoard.  In that order food is never problem for me
the rest of the game.

> But I’m thinking of taking a quick trip into the mines, since
> I should be able to handle very easily the monsters there, and can
> quickly get a bunch of XP, gold and gems (got to watch out for traps
> though). Is this a good plan?

There is a trade off between combat attack and combat avoidance.  Stay
on the attack and eventually you *will* hit a level that overwhelms you.

> Is it worth hanging around the higher D
> levels and trying to collect corpses to sac at the altar?

If you have a way to generate monsters probably.  If you don't have a
way of generating monsters probably not.  An altar level is a good place
for a major stash and it's easy to move among levels so at this point
you likely only want to use it for stash and trying on armor items to
increase your AC.

> Is it worth
> trying to skill up other weapons to allow for more weapon diversity or
> just stick to axe and hope for Cleaver?

Skill slot management is its whole separate topic.  A proper answer is
very long and is a skill issue for a later date at your current skill. 
When you enhance a skill have a reason for it - That's a reasonable
simple starting point.  Skill slots are a very limited resource so do
not just enhance a skill because it was offered.  In the long run have a
plan for how to use your slots.

> Is getting another pet a priority or not?

It depends.  Are you ready at this point to learn pet management?  It's
another one of those gigantic topics.


